All momenta below belong to a circle. In particular, \(k=\pi\) and \(k=-\pi\) are the same point and are counted once. A zero of the determinant is counted by its order of vanishing.

The only physical input used is the one allowed in the question: each independent real chiral Majorana field contributes \(1/2\) to the central charge of its chirality. The remaining arguments are finite-dimensional linear algebra and polynomial counting.

Theorem 1 (Bloch Hamiltonian, mode count, and left-right balance).

Write \(B=A(1)\). With Fourier convention
\[
\gamma_x=\frac1{\sqrt L}\sum_k e^{ikx}\gamma(k),
\qquad
\gamma(k)^\dagger=\gamma(-k),
\]
the Hamiltonian takes the form
\[
H=\frac14\sum_k\gamma(-k)^T h(k)\gamma(k),
\]
where
\[
h(k)=i\bigl(A(0)+Be^{ik}-B^Te^{-ik}\bigr).
\]
It satisfies
\[
h(k)^\dagger=h(k),
\qquad
h(-k)=-h(k)^*.
\]
Thus complex conjugation implements particle-hole symmetry, with square \(+1\).

Let \(M_+\) and \(M_-\) count all zero-energy band crossings with positive and negative velocity, respectively, including band multiplicities. Under the stated linear-crossing assumption,
\[
c_R=\frac{M_+}{2},
\qquad
c_L=\frac{M_-}{2}.
\]
Moreover,
\[
M_+=M_-,
\]
and the total multiplicity \(M\) of the zeros of \(\det h(k)\) satisfies
\[
M=M_++M_-.
\]
Consequently,
\[
\boxed{
c_L+c_R=\frac M2,
\qquad
c_L=c_R,
\qquad
c=\frac M4.
}
\]
The same assertions hold for a finite-period chain after forming its unit-cell Bloch matrix.

Proof.

Hermiticity follows from \(A(0)^T=-A(0)\) and the reality of \(A(0)\) and \(B\). Direct conjugation gives the stated particle-hole symmetry.

First, we identify determinant multiplicity with the number of vanishing bands. Fix a zero \(k_0\), put \(q=k-k_0\), and let
\[
P=\text{orthogonal projection onto }\ker h(k_0),
\qquad
m=\dim\ker h(k_0).
\]
In the decomposition \(P\oplus(1-P)\),
\[
h(k_0+q)=
\begin{pmatrix}
qV+O(q^2)&qW+O(q^2)\\
qW^\dagger+O(q^2)&D+O(q)
\end{pmatrix},
\]
where
\[
V=Ph'(k_0)P\big|_{\ker h(k_0)},
\]
and \(D\) is invertible.

The velocities of the \(m\) vanishing bands are the eigenvalues of \(V\). To see the first-order statement, insert \(E=qv+O(q^2)\) into the eigenvalue equation and eliminate the component in \(1-P\). That component is \(O(q)\) relative to the kernel component, so the kernel equation becomes
\[
q(V-vI)u+O(q^2)=0.
\]
Thus the leading slopes, including multiplicities, are the eigenvalues of \(V\). The linear-crossing assumption says that all of these eigenvalues are nonzero, so \(V\) is invertible.

The Schur-complement formula now gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\det h(k_0+q)
&=\det(D+O(q))\\
&\quad\times
\det\left(
qV+O(q^2)
-(qW+O(q^2))(D+O(q))^{-1}
(qW^\dagger+O(q^2))
\right)\\
&=q^m\det D\,\det V+O(q^{m+1}).
\end{aligned}
\]
The leading coefficient is nonzero. Therefore the determinant has a zero of order \(m\). If the entire matrix vanishes at \(k_0\), the same argument applies with the complementary block absent and \(\det D=1\).

Next, we count independent real fields. There are two cases.

1. A particle-hole orbit consisting of two distinct points, \(k_0\) and \(-k_0\).

   If a branch near \(k_0\) has
   \[
   E(k_0+q)=vq+O(q^2),
   \]
   its particle-hole partner near \(-k_0\) has
   \[
   \widetilde E(-k_0+q)
   =-E(k_0-q)
   =vq+O(q^2).
   \]
   The partners have the same velocity.

   The Fourier reality condition identifies their envelope operators as a complex field and its adjoint. They constitute one complex chiral fermion,
   \[
   \psi=\frac{\chi_1+i\chi_2}{\sqrt2},
   \]
   hence two real chiral Majoranas of the same chirality. Their central-charge contribution is \(1\).

   This orbit contributes two vanishing bands to the global count, one at each momentum. Thus its contribution is \(1/2\) per counted band. One must neither treat the two envelopes as independent complex fields nor divide their two-real-field contribution by two again.

2. A particle-hole fixed point, \(k_0=0\) or \(\pi\).

   Here \(h(k_0)\) is purely imaginary and antisymmetric, so its kernel has a real orthonormal basis. Differentiating particle-hole symmetry gives
   \[
   h'(-k)=h'(k)^*.
   \]
   At a fixed point, periodicity therefore makes \(h'(k_0)\) real. Since it is Hermitian, it is real symmetric. Consequently \(V\) can be diagonalized by a real orthogonal transformation on the kernel.

   Each resulting envelope field is self-adjoint. The carrier factor is \(1\) at \(0\) and \((-1)^x\) at \(\pi\), both real. Each vanishing band therefore supplies one real chiral Majorana, contributing \(1/2\).

Together these cases prove
\[
c_R=\frac{M_+}{2},
\qquad
c_L=\frac{M_-}{2},
\qquad
c_L+c_R=\frac M2.
\]

Finally, left-right balance follows from periodic spectral flow. Choose a starting momentum away from all zeros, and let \(N_-(k)\) be the number of strictly negative eigenvalues between crossings. At a crossing with \(m_+\) positive slopes and \(m_-\) negative slopes,
\[
N_-(k_0+0)-N_-(k_0-0)=m_--m_+.
\]
After one circuit of the Brillouin zone, the matrix returns to itself, so the total change is zero:
\[
0=M_--M_+.
\]
This proves \(c_L=c_R\).

Particle-hole symmetry alone would not establish this balance, because its paired branches have the same velocity. \(\square\)

Theorem 2 (translation-invariant nearest-neighbor bound).

Under the assumptions of case (a), with \(r=\operatorname{rank}A(1)\),
\[
\boxed{
M\le 2r,
\qquad
c\le\frac r2\le\frac n2.
}
\]

Proof.

Set \(z=e^{ik}\), \(D=iA(0)\), and \(T=iB\). Then
\[
h(z)=D+zT+z^{-1}T^\dagger,
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}T=\operatorname{rank}T^\dagger=r.
\]

Expand the determinant by multilinearity in its columns. A term selecting \(s\) columns from \(zT\), \(t\) columns from \(z^{-1}T^\dagger\), and the remaining columns from \(D\), carries the power \(z^{s-t}\).

If \(s>r\), the selected columns from \(T\) are linearly dependent, so that determinant term vanishes. The same holds if \(t>r\). Every nonzero term therefore obeys
\[
-r\le s-t\le r.
\]
Hence
\[
f(z):=\det h(z)=\sum_{\ell=-r}^{r}a_\ell z^\ell.
\]

The finite-zero assumption ensures that \(f\) is not identically zero. Thus
\[
P(z)=z^rf(z)
\]
is a nonzero ordinary polynomial of degree at most \(2r\).

Multiplication by \(z^r\) does not change multiplicities on the unit circle. Also, near \(k_0\),
\[
z-z_0=iz_0(k-k_0)+O((k-k_0)^2),
\qquad z_0=e^{ik_0}\ne0,
\]
so the order of a zero as a function of \(z\) equals its order as a function of \(k\). A nonzero polynomial of degree at most \(2r\) has at most \(2r\) roots counted with multiplicity, even before restricting to the unit circle. Therefore
\[
M\le2r.
\]
Theorem 1 gives \(c=M/4\), completing the proof. \(\square\)

Theorem 3 (the bound is independent of the period).

For a period-\(p\) chain with \(n\) Majoranas per original site,
\[
\boxed{c\le\frac n2.}
\]
More precisely, if \(\mathcal B\) is the coupling from one chosen unit cell to the next,
\[
c\le\frac{\operatorname{rank}\mathcal B}{2}.
\]

Proof.

Label sites as \(x=pj+\alpha\), with \(\alpha=0,\ldots,p-1\), and collect the \(np\) Majoranas in cell \(j\) into \(\Gamma_j\). The cell Bloch Hamiltonian is
\[
h_p(k)
=i\left(\mathcal A_0+\mathcal B e^{ik}
-\mathcal B^Te^{-ik}\right),
\]
where \(\mathcal A_0\) contains all onsite and within-cell couplings and is real antisymmetric.

For \(p\ge2\), original-lattice nearest-neighbor locality allows only the last site of cell \(j\) to couple to the first site of cell \(j+1\). Thus the only potentially nonzero \(n\times n\) block of \(\mathcal B\) is
\[
\mathcal B_{\alpha\beta}
=\delta_{\alpha,p-1}\delta_{\beta,0}B_{\mathrm{cut}}.
\]
It follows that
\[
r_{\mathrm{cell}}
:=\operatorname{rank}\mathcal B
=\operatorname{rank}B_{\mathrm{cut}}
\le n.
\]
For \(p=1\), this is just the matrix \(B\) of Theorem 2.

Although \(h_p\) has size \(np\times np\), the determinant expansion gives
\[
\det h_p(z)
=\sum_{\ell=-r_{\mathrm{cell}}}^{r_{\mathrm{cell}}}
b_\ell z^\ell.
\]
Indeed, selecting more than \(r_{\mathrm{cell}}\) columns from either inter-cell coefficient makes that determinant term vanish. Consequently,
\[
z^{r_{\mathrm{cell}}}\det h_p(z)
\]
is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most \(2r_{\mathrm{cell}}\le2n\). Therefore
\[
M\le2r_{\mathrm{cell}}\le2n,
\qquad
c=\frac M4\le\frac n2.
\]

Here \(k\) is conjugate to the cell index. Physical momentum is \(K=k/p\), so physical velocities are \(p\,\partial_kE\). This changes neither their signs nor the mode count. There is no division of the central charge by \(p\). \(\square\)

Proposition 4 (sharpness for every \(n\)).

For any positive integer \(n\), the bound is attained by
\[
H=it\sum_x\sum_{a=1}^n
\gamma_{x,a}\gamma_{x+1,a},
\qquad t\ne0.
\]

Proof.

In the normalization of the question,
\[
A(0)=0,
\qquad
A(1)=2tI_n,
\qquad
h(k)=-4t\sin k\,I_n.
\]
There are two zero-energy points, \(0\) and \(\pi\), each with multiplicity \(n\). All slopes are nonzero:
\[
h'(0)=-4tI_n,
\qquad
h'(\pi)=4tI_n.
\]
Thus
\[
M=2n,
\qquad
c_L=c_R=\frac n2,
\qquad
c=\frac n2.
\]

For even \(n=2m\), pair the Majoranas into ordinary complex fermions,
\[
f_{x,j}=\frac{\gamma_{x,2j-1}+i\gamma_{x,2j}}2.
\]
The same example becomes
\[
H=2it\sum_{x,j}
\left(
f_{x,j}^\dagger f_{x+1,j}
-f_{x+1,j}^\dagger f_{x,j}
\right).
\]
It is a charge-conserving model with \(m\) complex orbitals per site and \(d=2^m\).

For odd \(n=2m+1\), pair the first \(2m\) Majoranas in the same way and retain the remaining one-Majorana chain. This gives
\[
c=m+\frac12=\frac n2.
\]
For finite periodic systems with odd \(n\), take even \(L\), so the total number of Majoranas is even. This restriction does not affect the thermodynamic mode count. \(\square\)

Charge-conserving fermions.

For \(n_c\) complex orbitals per site, write the physical single-particle matrix as
\[
q(k)=M+Te^{ik}+T^\dagger e^{-ik},
\qquad M=M^\dagger.
\]
Let \(F\) be the total number of its zero-energy crossings, counted with multiplicity and assuming nonzero slopes.

Each crossing of this physical complex-fermion matrix supplies one complex chiral field. Therefore
\[
c_L+c_R=F,
\qquad
c=\frac F2.
\]
The same column argument gives
\[
F\le2\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb C}T,
\]
and spectral flow gives equal numbers of positive and negative slopes. Hence
\[
\boxed{
c_L=c_R,
\qquad
c\le\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb C}T
\le n_c=\log_2d.
}
\]
For a period-\(p\) model, the inter-cell hopping matrix has rank at most \(n_c\), so the same bound holds.

This is consistent with the Majorana count. In Nambu notation the doubled matrix is
\[
h_{\mathrm{BdG}}(k)=
\begin{pmatrix}
q(k)&0\\
0&-q(-k)^T
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Its determinant has total zero multiplicity \(2F\), including when zeros from the two blocks coincide. The Majorana formula then gives
\[
c=\frac{2F}{4}=\frac F2.
\]

Non-Hermitian models.

The Laurent-degree argument is algebraic and survives without Hermiticity. For a general matrix \(D+zT_++z^{-1}T_-\), the polynomial root bound is
\[
M\le\operatorname{rank}T_++\operatorname{rank}T_-,
\]
provided its determinant is not identically zero. For a complex antisymmetric Majorana coupling, the forward and backward coefficients still have equal rank, giving \(M\le2r\).

The identification with central charge does not follow from this algebraic statement. Hermitian spectral flow and the assumed free real-Majorana interpretation are no longer available in general. Exceptional points can also invalidate the linear-mode description.

Under the usual nonunitary CFT finite-size convention,
\[
c_{\mathrm{eff}}
=\frac{c_L+c_R}{2}-12(h_{\min}+\bar h_{\min}),
\]
which reduces to \(c-24h_{\min}\) when the left and right quantities agree. This convention and a suitable conformal scaling limit are additional assumptions. The determinant bound alone does not establish a bound on \(c_{\mathrm{eff}}\).

Linear Dirac crossings and quadratic touchings.

If two bands of the Majorana Bloch matrix vanish at the same point with nonzero slopes \(v_1,v_2\), then
\[
\det h(k_0+q)
=Cv_1v_2q^2+O(q^3),
\qquad C\ne0.
\]
The point counts twice. Opposite slopes do not cancel the determinant multiplicity.

At a particle-hole fixed point, an opposite-slope pair gives one right-moving and one left-moving real Majorana:
\[
\Delta c_R=\Delta c_L=\frac12.
\]
At a nonfixed point, its distinct particle-hole partner must also be included. The combined opposite-slope pair at each of the two momenta gives
\[
\Delta c_R=\Delta c_L=1.
\]

A quadratic touching instead has \(E\sim aq^2\), zero group velocity, and dynamical exponent \(2\). The Laurent-degree bound still counts determinant zeros, but their multiplicity no longer counts linearly dispersing chiral fields. Such a touching lies outside the conformal assumption, so \(c=M/4\) cannot be applied to it.

Comparison with \(d-1\).

For \(d=2^m\), with integer \(m\ge0\),
\[
\log_2d=m\le2^m-1=d-1.
\]
For \(m\ge1\), the binomial expansion gives
\[
2^m=1+m+\sum_{j=2}^m\binom mj.
\]
Equality holds at \(m=1\), and the sum is positive for \(m\ge2\). The trivial case \(m=0\) also gives equality. Thus equality holds only for
\[
d=1,\ 2,
\]
or only \(d=2\) if a nontrivial local Hilbert space is required.

The explicit checks requested in item 1 follow.

1. Critical transverse-field Ising chain, \(n=2\).

   Use the spin normalization
   \[
   H_{\mathrm{spin}}
   =-\frac12\sum_x
   \left(J\sigma_x^x\sigma_{x+1}^x+g\sigma_x^z\right).
   \]
   Its Jordan-Wigner Majorana representation, apart from the boundary-sector choice that is immaterial in the thermodynamic limit, is
   \[
   H=\frac i2\sum_x
   \left(g\,a_xb_x+J\,b_xa_{x+1}\right).
   \]
   Hence
   \[
   A(0)=
   \begin{pmatrix}0&g\\-g&0\end{pmatrix},
   \qquad
   B=
   \begin{pmatrix}0&0\\J&0\end{pmatrix},
   \]
   and
   \[
   h(k)=
   \begin{pmatrix}
   0&i(g-Je^{-ik})\\
   i(-g+Je^{ik})&0
   \end{pmatrix}.
   \]
   Therefore
   \[
   \det h(k)
   =-\left(g^2+J^2-2gJ\cos k\right).
   \]

   At \(g=J>0\),
   \[
   \det h(k)=-4J^2\sin^2(k/2).
   \]
   There is one zero on the momentum circle, at \(k=0\), of order two. Near it,
   \[
   h(q)=-Jq\,\sigma_x+O(q^2),
   \]
   so the two velocities are \(+J\) and \(-J\).

   The globally ordered energies
   \[
   E_\pm(k)=\pm2J|\sin(k/2)|
   \]
   have a cusp because the ordering exchanges the crossing branches. The local crossing branches have nonzero linear slopes, as the matrix expansion shows.

   Both modes occur at a particle-hole fixed point and are real Majoranas. Thus
   \[
   M=2,\qquad
   c_R=c_L=\frac12,\qquad
   \boxed{c=\frac12}.
   \]
   Also \(\operatorname{rank}B=1\), so this saturates the rank-dependent bound.

2. XX chain at generic filling, \(n=2\).

   Write its number-conserving fermion Hamiltonian as
   \[
   H=-J\sum_x(f_x^\dagger f_{x+1}+f_{x+1}^\dagger f_x)
   -\mu\sum_x\left(f_x^\dagger f_x-\frac12\right),
   \]
   with \(J>0\) and \(|\mu|<2J\). Define
   \[
   f_x=\frac{a_x+ib_x}{2}.
   \]
   The identities
   \[
   f_x^\dagger f_x-\frac12=\frac i2a_xb_x,
   \qquad
   f_x^\dagger f_{x+1}+f_{x+1}^\dagger f_x
   =\frac i2(a_xb_{x+1}-b_xa_{x+1})
   \]
   give
   \[
   A(0)=
   \begin{pmatrix}0&-\mu\\\mu&0\end{pmatrix},
   \qquad
   B=
   \begin{pmatrix}0&-J\\J&0\end{pmatrix}.
   \]
   Set
   \[
   \varepsilon(k)=-\mu-2J\cos k.
   \]
   Then
   \[
   h(k)=
   \begin{pmatrix}
   0&i\varepsilon(k)\\
   -i\varepsilon(k)&0
   \end{pmatrix},
   \qquad
   \det h(k)=-\varepsilon(k)^2.
   \]

   The zeros are
   \[
   k=\pm k_F,
   \qquad
   k_F=\arccos\!\left(-\frac{\mu}{2J}\right)\in(0,\pi).
   \]
   Each determinant zero has order two, because
   \[
   \varepsilon'(\pm k_F)=\pm2J\sin k_F\ne0.
   \]
   The two eigenvalue branches of \(h\) have opposite slopes at each point. Thus
   \[
   M=4,\qquad M_+=M_-=2.
   \]

   Particle-hole symmetry pairs the positive-slope branch at one momentum with the positive-slope branch at the other, producing one complex right mover. The negative-slope branches produce one complex left mover. Consequently,
   \[
   c_R=c_L=1,
   \qquad
   \boxed{c=1}.
   \]
   Here \(\operatorname{rank}B=2\), so the \(n/2\) bound is attained.

3. One Majorana per site, \(n=1\).

   For
   \[
   H=it\sum_x\gamma_x\gamma_{x+1},
   \qquad t\ne0,
   \]
   the normalization \(H=(i/4)\gamma A\gamma\) requires
   \[
   A(1)=2t,\qquad A(-1)=-2t.
   \]
   Therefore
   \[
   h(k)=i(2te^{ik}-2te^{-ik})=-4t\sin k.
   \]
   There are two simple zeros, \(0\) and \(\pi\), with velocities
   \[
   v_0=-4t,\qquad v_\pi=4t.
   \]
   Both are particle-hole fixed points, and each supplies one real chiral Majorana. For \(t>0\), the \(0\) mode is left-moving and the \(\pi\) mode is right-moving; changing the sign of \(t\) exchanges them. Hence
   \[
   M=2,\qquad
   c_R=c_L=\frac12,\qquad
   \boxed{c=\frac12}.
   \]

Thus the bound is sharp for every \(n\), and period enlargement leaves it unchanged. The determinant-to-central-charge identification requires the stated linear, Hermitian free-fermion scaling limit; the polynomial degree bound itself does not.

